---
// Moved verbatim from privacy.html. Astro bundles the imported CSS into the page's <head>.
import "../style.css";
import "../legal.css";

const site = Astro.site!.origin;
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>Privacy Policy · relayrunner</title>
    <meta
      name="description"
      content="What the hosted RelayRunner service collects, why, who it's shared with and how to have it deleted."
    />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#ffffff" media="(prefers-color-scheme: light)" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#0b0d10" media="(prefers-color-scheme: dark)" />
    <link rel="canonical" href={`${site}/privacy`} />
    <link rel="icon" href="/favicon.png" type="image/png" />
    <link rel="apple-touch-icon" href="/apple-touch-icon.png" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <a class="skip" href="#main">Skip to content</a>

    <header class="site-header">
      <div class="wrap header-row">
        <a class="brand" href="/" aria-label="relayrunner home">
          <span class="brand-symbol" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          relayrunner
        </a>
        <nav aria-label="Primary">
          <ul class="nav-links">
            <li><a href="/terms">Terms</a></li>
            <li>
              <a class="btn-ghost btn-sm" href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner"
                >GitHub</a
              >
            </li>
          </ul>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </header>

    <main id="main" class="wrap legal">
      <h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
      <p class="updated">Last updated November 12, 2026</p>

      <p class="intro">
        RelayRunner is an open source project. This policy covers the hosted service its maintainers
        run: the dashboard at app.relayrunner.app, its API and webhooks, the RelayRunner GitHub
        Apps, RelayRunner's hosted runners, the <code>relayrunner</code> CLI when it talks to that
        service, and this website. "RelayRunner", "we" and "us" mean the maintainers who operate it.
        If you run RelayRunner yourself from the
        <a href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner">source code</a>, you operate your copy
        and this policy doesn't apply to it.
      </p>

      <section class="summary" aria-labelledby="summary-h">
        <h2 id="summary-h">The short version</h2>
        <ul>
          <li>
            We collect what we need to route your CI jobs: your GitHub profile, the organizations
            and repositories you connect, details about workflow runs and jobs, and the machines you
            connect.
          </li>
          <li>
            We don't sell your data, show ads, use third-party analytics, or train AI models on it.
          </li>
          <li>We don't keep your source code or your job logs.</li>
          <li>
            Email <a href="mailto:privacy@relayrunner.app">privacy@relayrunner.app</a> to get a copy
            of your data or have it deleted.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <h2>What we collect</h2>

      <h3>Your account</h3>
      <p>
        You sign in with GitHub. GitHub gives us your user ID, username, name, email address and
        avatar, and an access token that we store to keep you signed in. For each sign-in session we
        record the IP address and browser user agent, so we can spot sessions you don't recognize.
      </p>

      <h3>Workspaces and members</h3>
      <p>
        We store your workspaces, one for each GitHub account with the RelayRunner GitHub App
        installed, and their members and roles, which come from GitHub.
      </p>

      <h3>GitHub organizations and repositories</h3>
      <p>
        When you install the RelayRunner GitHub App, GitHub tells us about the installation and the
        repositories it can see: names, IDs and whether each is public or private. If you add an
        organization's existing self-hosted runners, we store their names and labels. Where the App
        has permission, we read the organization's GitHub Actions usage report (minutes and amounts
        per repository) and budget settings to show on the Usage page.
      </p>

      <h3>Workflow runs and jobs</h3>
      <p>
        GitHub sends us events about workflow runs and jobs in connected repositories. From them we
        store the repository, workflow and job names, the run's title (usually a commit message or
        pull request title), branch, commit SHA, pull request number, the GitHub username that
        triggered the run, runner labels, status, result and timings, along with where we routed
        each job and why. We use the timings to report minutes used and saved. We don't receive your
        job logs or your repository secrets.
      </p>

      <h3>Connected machines</h3>
      <p>
        When you run <code>relayrunner connect</code>, we store the machine's name, operating system
        and version, CPU architecture, model, chip, core count and memory, when it was last online,
        and a hash of its access token. Jobs that run on your machine use GitHub's own runner, which
        talks to GitHub directly. Their code, secrets and logs don't pass through us.
      </p>

      <h3>Repository contents, for lint checks only</h3>
      <p>
        If you install the separate, optional RelayRunner Checks App and turn on lint, we download
        each pull request commit's source archive from GitHub, lint it in a container with no
        internet access, post the results to GitHub as check runs (file paths, line numbers and
        messages), and then stop the container. We don't keep a copy of your code. We also read your
        <code>relayrunner.yml</code> to see which checks you've turned on.
      </p>

      <h3>Hosted runners</h3>
      <p>
        If a job is routed to a RelayRunner-hosted runner, it runs in a single-use container on
        Cloudflare. As on any runner, the job's code and secrets are inside that container while the
        job runs. The container is discarded afterwards.
      </p>

      <h3>Logs</h3>
      <p>
        Our servers keep request logs, including IP addresses, for debugging and abuse prevention.
        GitHub webhook deliveries we fail to process are kept for up to 30 days so we can replay
        them.
      </p>

      <h3>This website and cookies</h3>
      <p>
        relayrunner.app sets no cookies and runs no analytics. The dashboard uses one cookie to keep
        you signed in. That's the only cookie we set.
      </p>

      <h2>How we use it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          To run the service: signing you in, routing jobs, showing activity and usage, and posting
          check results.
        </li>
        <li>
          To send service email: a welcome message and security notices when a machine connects
          or signs in from somewhere new. We don't send marketing email.
        </li>
        <li>To keep the service secure, fix problems and prevent abuse.</li>
        <li>To meet legal obligations.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Who we share it with</h2>
      <p>We don't sell personal information. We share it only as follows.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Cloudflare</strong> hosts the service, its database, storage, containers and logs,
          and sends our email.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>GitHub.</strong> RelayRunner is built on GitHub. We call GitHub's API for your
          installations, for example to register runners and post check runs.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Other runner providers.</strong> When a job uses a label for another provider,
          such as Blacksmith, that provider's own GitHub App runs it under your agreement with them.
          We don't send them your data.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Your workspace.</strong> Members of a workspace can see its machines, runners,
          activity and usage.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Maintainers.</strong> The maintainers who operate the service can see accounts and
          workspaces when they need to, to support you or keep the service running.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>When required.</strong> If the law requires it, or to protect the service or its
          users.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A new operator.</strong> If the hosted service moves to a new operator, such as an
          organization formed to run it, your data moves with it and this policy keeps applying.
        </li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How long we keep it</h2>
      <p>
        We keep your data while your account or workspace uses the service. Failed webhook
        deliveries are deleted after 30 days, and request logs after a few days. When you ask us to
        delete your account, we delete your data except where we must keep it to meet a legal
        obligation.
      </p>

      <h2>Your choices and rights</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          Email <a href="mailto:privacy@relayrunner.app">privacy@relayrunner.app</a> to see,
          correct, export or delete your data. We honor these requests for everyone, wherever you
          live.
        </li>
        <li>
          Uninstall the RelayRunner GitHub Apps or revoke RelayRunner's access in your GitHub
          settings at any time.
        </li>
        <li>
          Disconnect machines from the dashboard or by stopping <code>relayrunner connect</code>.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If you're in the EEA, UK or Switzerland: we process your data to provide the service you
        signed up for, for our legitimate interests in keeping it secure and working, and to meet
        legal obligations. You can object to processing, and you can complain to your local data
        protection authority.
      </p>

      <h2>Security</h2>
      <p>
        Traffic is encrypted in transit, machine tokens are stored only as hashes, and GitHub
        webhooks are verified by signature. No system is perfectly secure. Please report
        vulnerabilities to <a href="mailto:security@relayrunner.app">security@relayrunner.app</a>.
      </p>

      <h2>Where your data is processed</h2>
      <p>
        Cloudflare runs the service on its global network, so your data may be processed in the
        United States and other countries.
      </p>

      <h2>Children</h2>
      <p>RelayRunner needs a GitHub account and isn't meant for children under 16.</p>

      <h2>Changes</h2>
      <p>If we change this policy, we'll post the new version here and update the date above.</p>

      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>
        Privacy questions and requests:
        <a href="mailto:privacy@relayrunner.app">privacy@relayrunner.app</a>. Security reports:
        <a href="mailto:security@relayrunner.app">security@relayrunner.app</a>. Anything else:
        <a href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner/issues">GitHub issues</a>.
      </p>
    </main>

    <footer class="site-footer">
      <div class="wrap footer-row">
        <a class="brand brand-sm" href="/">
          <span class="brand-symbol" aria-hidden="true"></span>
          relayrunner
        </a>
        <ul>
          <li><a href="/docs">Docs</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/relayrunner/relayrunner">GitHub</a></li>
          <li><a href="https://github.com/apps/relayrunner">GitHub App</a></li>
          <li><a href="/privacy" aria-current="page">Privacy</a></li>
          <li><a href="/terms">Terms</a></li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
